\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Connected correlations and the long periodic limit}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Use the positive normalized vector #{\Omega} and
 #{\lambda_0} of \ref{fdip-00N6}, and put
 #{S=T_{\mathrm{phys}}/\lambda_0} on the
 \vocabk{physical Hilbert space}{fdip-0083}.
 For any bounded self-adjoint operator #{O}, set
##{O_c=O-\langle\Omega,O\Omega\rangle I,\qquad
 v=O_c\Omega.}
Let #{P_0=|\Omega\rangle\langle\Omega|}; for each distinct
 eigenvalue #{r\in(0,1)} of #{S}, let #{E_r} be its
 orthogonal spectral projection. The vacuum connected correlation
 is, for every integer #{n\ge0},
##{C_O(n)=\langle\Omega,O_cS^nO_c\Omega\rangle
 =\sum_{0<r<1}r^n\|E_rv\|^2.}
The sum is absolutely convergent, including #{n=0}.
 For integers #{N>n},
##{\lim_{N\to\infty}
 \frac{\operatorname{Tr}(O_cS^nO_cS^{N-n})}
      {\operatorname{Tr}(S^N)}=C_O(n).}
If the physical space has dimension greater than one, its
 largest excited eigenvalue #{r_*} satisfies #{r_*<1}. With
 the time step #{a_t>0} of \ref{fdip-0085}, put
 #{\Delta=-a_t^{-1}\log r_*>0}. Then
##{0\le C_O(n)\le e^{-na_t\Delta}\|v\|^2.}
If the physical space is one-dimensional, #{C_O(n)=0} and
 there is no excited eigenvalue.}

\proof{
\p{Self-adjointness makes #{\langle\Omega,O\Omega\rangle}
 real and #{v\perp\Omega}. Compactness, positivity and
 injectivity from \ref{fdip-0083}, together with vacuum
 simplicity, give an orthogonal decomposition into the vacuum
 line and the positive excited eigenspaces. Parseval gives
 #{\sum_r\|E_rv\|^2=\|v\|^2}; applying #{S^n} on
 each eigenspace proves the correlation formula and convergence.}

\p{For integers #{m\ge1}, positivity and the same spectral
 decomposition give
##{\|S^m-P_0\|_1=\sum_{0<r<1}(\operatorname{rank}E_r)r^m
 \longrightarrow0.}
The summands are dominated by the summable trace series for
 #{S}; each tends to zero. Let #{A=O_cS^nO_c}, a bounded
 operator of norm at most #{\|O_c\|^2}. The trace bound
 #{|\operatorname{Tr}(AB)|\le\|A\|\|B\|_1} gives
##{\operatorname{Tr}(AS^{N-n})\longrightarrow
 \operatorname{Tr}(AP_0)=\langle\Omega,A\Omega\rangle.}
Also #{\operatorname{Tr}(S^N)\to1}, proving the ratio limit.
 All traces are defined since #{N-n\ge1}.}

\p{If the vacuum complement is nonzero, the restriction of
 #{S} to it is a nonzero positive compact operator. Its norm
 #{r_*} is an eigenvalue. Simplicity excludes #{r_*=1};
 bounding each #{r^n} by #{r_*^n} proves the estimate.
 In dimension one every bounded operator is scalar, so #{v=0}.}
}

\p{When #{O=M_f} for a bounded real gauge-invariant spatial
 function, \ref{fdip-0083} identifies the finite trace ratio
 with its two insertion integral in the periodic link law.
 At #{n=0} the insertion is #{(f-\langle\Omega,M_f\Omega\rangle)^2}.
 A general bounded self-adjoint operator has the trace formula
 above without being a function of spatial links. The gap
 #{\Delta} concerns the specified finite graph and transfer data;
 no uniform bound as volume or spacing changes is asserted.}
